<TopicPage slug="subjects/law/intermediate/how-a-case-moves-through-court">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    A lawsuit is <strong>a structured argument with checkpoints</strong> — each stage a filter
    designed to resolve cases that shouldn't need trial. Most people imagine the courtroom; in
    reality ~95% of civil matters settle during discovery, and appeals rarely revisit facts. The
    procedural map explains where cases actually end.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the five stages, from filing to precedent" widget="equationStep" config={caseCfg} />

  <h2>Procedural concepts with real consequences</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Jurisdiction first:</strong> which court, which territory, which subject matter — the threshold question that can end a case before its merits are heard.</li>
    <li><strong>Statutes of limitation:</strong> claims expire. Deadlines are jurisdictional — a valid claim filed a week late is dead.</li>
    <li><strong>Standard of review on appeal:</strong> factual findings get deference; legal questions get fresh review. Appellate strategy is choosing which kind of error you claim.</li>
    <li><strong>Costs follow the case:</strong> many systems make losers pay winners' costs — a structural pressure toward settlement that shapes everything upstream.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> standard civil procedure texts; Glanville Williams, Learning the Law; Zuckerman on Civil Procedure.</p>
</TopicPage>
